00:49:38.830 --> 00:49:40.960
de-Nazification was one of them,

00:49:40.960 --> 00:49:43.240
but so was decartelization
and deconcentration

00:49:44.686 --> 00:49:45.763
of German industry,

00:49:45.763 --> 00:49:47.280
which in the end does not really,

00:49:47.280 --> 00:49:49.763
it doesn't really happen
to a large extent.

00:49:50.890 --> 00:49:54.120
It's only, IG Farben is
a very prominent example

00:49:54.120 --> 00:49:57.560
of at the time the largest chemicals

00:49:57.560 --> 00:50:01.660
and pharmaceuticals conglomerate
in the world split up

00:50:01.660 --> 00:50:04.528
in what is today, BASF and Bayer,

00:50:04.528 --> 00:50:07.250
still two of the large
companies in the world

00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:11.263
in pharmaceuticals and
chemicals, respectively.

00:50:12.310 --> 00:50:15.560
But for the most part, of
course, the cold war emerges,

00:50:15.560 --> 00:50:20.560
and, you know, initially Harry Truman

00:50:21.270 --> 00:50:22.530
and then Ike Eisenhower,

00:50:22.530 --> 00:50:25.610
but particularly Truman
makes the policy decision

00:50:25.610 --> 00:50:28.210
that we, that United States government

00:50:28.210 --> 00:50:30.570
needs a viable democratic,

00:50:30.570 --> 00:50:34.910
or economically viable and
democratic West Germany

00:50:34.910 --> 00:50:38.600
as a bulwark against an
encroaching Soviet Union,

00:50:38.600 --> 00:50:41.000
and all the aspirations
and ambitions that they had

00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:46.000
for the reform of German
society and de-Nazification,

00:50:46.050 --> 00:50:48.160
it all quickly goes out the window,

00:50:48.160 --> 00:50:52.760
and the aim, the overarching
aim becomes the build up

00:50:52.760 --> 00:50:56.910
of West German society

00:50:56.910 --> 00:51:01.270
as a democratic and a
capitalist state, yeah.

00:51:01.270 --> 00:51:04.429
- So some people are asking, you know,

00:51:04.429 --> 00:51:06.910
you really focus on kind of
heavy industry, I would say,

00:51:06.910 --> 00:51:09.126
in the book, but, you know,

00:51:09.126 --> 00:51:11.770
you clearly could have talked
about, you know, some people,

00:51:11.770 --> 00:51:12.940
someone raised the question

00:51:12.940 --> 00:51:17.100
of like the entertainment
business, you know,

00:51:17.100 --> 00:51:20.250
and I imagine that there
were kind of similar stories

00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:22.930
going on in all different
aspects of, you know,

00:51:22.930 --> 00:51:25.000
all different areas of the German economy.

00:51:25.000 --> 00:51:27.340
Is that true would you say, or?

00:51:27.340 --> 00:51:30.260
- Well, listen, I mean,
I picked the families,

00:51:30.260 --> 00:51:31.900
I picked the business families of those

00:51:31.900 --> 00:51:35.590
that are still most
relevant today in European,

00:51:35.590 --> 00:51:38.180
in German, European and global business,

00:51:38.180 --> 00:51:40.650
so that it could have, you know,

00:51:40.650 --> 00:51:45.650
I think in a sense the
businesses that I chose,

00:51:47.880 --> 00:51:49.550
and I think that's why
they're also so emblematic

00:51:49.550 --> 00:51:53.020
or so exemplary of the larger,
wider business community

00:51:53.020 --> 00:51:56.090
and their thoughts is
because they do range.

00:51:56.090 --> 00:52:01.090
I mean sure, Gunther Quandt,
Friedrich Flick, big industry,

00:52:01.480 --> 00:52:04.466
but one would say, you know, Porsche car,

00:52:04.466 --> 00:52:09.466
and then of course the,
von Finck is in finance

00:52:13.230 --> 00:52:16.440
and the Oetkers are in
consumer goods at that time.

00:52:16.440 --> 00:52:17.729
- Yeah.

00:52:17.729 --> 00:52:20.260
- Right, so it does, it does give,

00:52:20.260 --> 00:52:23.340
the two main characters are
indeed in heavy industry,

00:52:23.340 --> 00:52:25.922
but I wanted to focus on those families

00:52:25.922 --> 00:52:27.020
that are relevant today, so what,

00:52:27.020 --> 00:52:30.940
regardless of what industries
they were in at the time

00:52:30.940 --> 00:52:32.930
of the Nazi era.

00:52:32.930 --> 00:52:36.760
- Yeah, and I, you know,
your opening kind of scene

00:52:36.760 --> 00:52:39.870
where these rich Germans are summoned

00:52:39.870 --> 00:52:42.270
to a meeting with Hitler
is incredibly chilling

00:52:42.270 --> 00:52:43.270
where like you want, you know,

00:52:43.270 --> 00:52:45.160
there's a lot, you know,
how are they gonna react?

00:52:45.160 --> 00:52:47.025
And they, you know,

00:52:47.025 --> 00:52:49.500
are more than willing to help finance his.

00:52:49.500 --> 00:52:52.120
So here, a bunch of people have, you know,

00:52:52.120 --> 00:52:55.140
put forward a really interesting
question that I had too,

00:52:55.140 --> 00:52:59.210
which is, you know, you end the
book on a maybe hopeful note

00:52:59.210 --> 00:53:01.190
saying, you know, we will do better.

00:53:01.190 --> 00:53:02.525
- Right.

00:53:02.525 --> 00:53:06.290
- But a lot of people are asking
what that would look like.

00:53:06.290 --> 00:53:10.561
So what do you hope
your book accomplishes?

00:53:10.561 --> 00:53:15.561
What kind of reckoning are
you hoping for with this book?

00:53:15.847 --> 00:53:17.970
- I mean, at the bare minimum is this,

00:53:17.970 --> 00:53:19.440
and that's also the idea I went into.

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:21.687
I mean the book is an argument

00:53:21.687 --> 00:53:22.600
in favor of historical transparency.

00:53:22.600 --> 00:53:24.460
And I think at the bare minimum,

00:53:24.460 --> 00:53:26.020
these families should be

00:53:27.210 --> 00:53:29.660
with the massive consumer
brands that they control

00:53:29.660 --> 00:53:34.660
should be outwardly
transparent about the histories

00:53:35.927 --> 00:53:39.765
of the vaunted patriarchs

00:53:39.765 --> 00:53:41.460
that they called their foundations
and global headquarters

00:53:41.460 --> 00:53:44.410
and brands and media prizes after.

00:53:44.410 --> 00:53:47.550
I mean, I think that is a bare
minimum that we can expect.

00:53:47.550 --> 00:53:50.390
And if they don't wanna
do that, then, you know,

00:53:50.390 --> 00:53:52.030
I think they should rename.

00:53:52.030 --> 00:53:53.003
They should rename.

00:53:54.790 --> 00:53:56.221
That is what a reckoning,

00:53:56.221 --> 00:53:59.410
that is what an honest and
transparent reckoning looks like

00:53:59.410 --> 00:54:02.180
to me, because I also think, you know,

00:54:02.180 --> 00:54:05.840
one learns from history by
showing the good and the bad.

00:54:05.840 --> 00:54:07.690
And if you just celebrate
somebody's business success,

00:54:07.690 --> 00:54:09.845
but leave out their war crimes,

00:54:09.845 --> 00:54:12.250
especially today, when we live
in an age of such, you know,

00:54:12.250 --> 00:54:15.476
disinformation all across
the board, you know,

00:54:15.476 --> 00:54:18.210
these families particularly
have a responsibility,

00:54:18.210 --> 00:54:21.470
not only to the consumers,
but also to history

00:54:21.470 --> 00:54:22.750
to be transparent about it.

00:54:22.750 --> 00:54:25.420
- And, you know, you cite,
I forget which one it was,

00:54:25.420 --> 00:54:29.580
but like a young woman now who
just recently gave a speech

00:54:29.580 --> 00:54:31.760
that where she's just completely,

00:54:31.760 --> 00:54:34.380
it's not even like they're
trying to hide it anymore.

00:54:34.380 --> 00:54:36.950
I mean, she's almost like,
you know, like the past,

00:54:36.950 --> 00:54:38.540
what does it matter?

00:54:38.540 --> 00:54:39.460
I'm rich.

00:54:39.460 --> 00:54:40.854
- Yeah.

00:54:40.854 --> 00:54:43.447
- So I, you know, and.

00:54:43.447 --> 00:54:44.652
- It's very troubling,

00:54:44.652 --> 00:54:45.610
which is a very troubling indication

00:54:45.610 --> 00:54:47.150
for where Germany is headed.

00:54:47.150 --> 00:54:48.573
- Yeah.

00:54:48.573 --> 00:54:50.117
- She spoke, she spoke off
the cuff, but, you know,

00:54:50.117 --> 00:54:55.117
I mean kind of the blatant lack
of any historical knowledge

00:54:56.300 --> 00:54:58.250
and kind of any sense of morality

00:54:58.250 --> 00:55:00.839
that Verena Bahlsen displayed,

00:55:00.839 --> 00:55:02.139
not only during that speech,

00:55:02.139 --> 00:55:04.420
but particularly in the follow
up comments that she gave

00:55:04.420 --> 00:55:07.375
to build the German--

00:55:07.375 --> 00:55:08.208
- Right, it's a food company.

00:55:08.208 --> 00:55:09.454
Like they have a.

00:55:09.454 --> 00:55:10.678
- It's Bahlsen, Bahlsen,
it's a very famous.

00:55:10.678 --> 00:55:12.355
- Bahlsen, yeah.

00:55:12.355 --> 00:55:14.190
But there was some shaming
and kind of boycotting

00:55:14.190 --> 00:55:16.437
it seemed like after
her speech, so there's.

00:55:16.437 --> 00:55:18.210
- Sure, and I saw,

00:55:18.210 --> 00:55:21.200
I end the book all the way in the back

00:55:21.200 --> 00:55:23.110
in the notes of sources,

00:55:23.110 --> 00:55:26.553
where she gives this
interview in September, 2021

00:55:26.553 --> 00:55:29.290
to the Deutsche Society
about how, you know,

00:55:29.290 --> 00:55:32.170
she now forces her
family to talk about it.

00:55:32.170 --> 00:55:33.560
And therefore she's forcing them

00:55:33.560 --> 00:55:37.170
to be more transparent about
it, but it's still difficult.

00:55:37.170 --> 00:55:39.210
So, I mean, I do, I am, I mean,

00:55:39.210 --> 00:55:42.530
to get back to the previous
question, you know,

00:55:42.530 --> 00:55:46.199
I am hopeful about my generation,
even though some show,

00:55:46.199 --> 00:55:51.199
you know, a blatant lack of
knowledge about that period,

00:55:52.520 --> 00:55:54.240
you know, particularly those German,

00:55:54.240 --> 00:55:57.713
and particularly those who
are end up being the heirs.

00:55:58.694 --> 00:56:02.334
Because I do think in
the four and a half years

00:56:02.334 --> 00:56:06.430
that I lived in Germany and
I know that in the op-ed too,

00:56:06.430 --> 00:56:09.250
in the essay too, in the
New York Times is that I,

00:56:09.250 --> 00:56:11.890
everybody is extremely aware

00:56:11.890 --> 00:56:16.510
and is extremely reflective and is trying

00:56:16.510 --> 00:56:20.277
to make a better Germany, you know?

00:56:20.277 --> 00:56:22.877
And of course with its
political and economic might,

00:56:24.280 --> 00:56:27.145
you know, is extremely important.

00:56:27.145 --> 00:56:28.602
- Yeah, I mean,

00:56:28.602 --> 00:56:31.160
one of the kind of fascinating
things is you described

00:56:31.160 --> 00:56:34.840
that many of these families
have hired historians

00:56:34.840 --> 00:56:37.060
to uncover the truth about their past,

00:56:37.060 --> 00:56:40.240
but somehow these histories wind up

00:56:40.240 --> 00:56:43.770
almost seeming like part of
the cover up in some ways.

00:56:43.770 --> 00:56:44.603
I mean, they're kind.

00:56:44.603 --> 00:56:47.340
- Yes, unintentionally, in
some cases, in most cases,

00:56:47.340 --> 00:56:52.340
completely unintentionally
and in some intentionally.

00:56:52.590 --> 00:56:54.380
- Yeah, I mean, you
said they were actually

00:56:54.380 --> 00:56:56.320
kind of good historians often,

00:56:56.320 --> 00:56:57.670
but then the book comes out,

00:56:57.670 --> 00:56:59.620
and they think they've done
everything they need to do,

00:56:59.620 --> 00:57:01.050
and then nobody, you know.

00:57:01.050 --> 00:57:02.890
- The historians and
their research, I mean,

00:57:02.890 --> 00:57:05.210
it's also insulting to the
historians and their researchers

00:57:05.210 --> 00:57:07.310
will work on this project
for three, four years.

00:57:07.310 --> 00:57:09.815
And then these studies come out,

00:57:09.815 --> 00:57:12.850
and they are only published
in dense academic German,

00:57:12.850 --> 00:57:14.300
and then, you know, nothing changes.

00:57:14.300 --> 00:57:16.410
And then the findings,
you can't find anywhere

00:57:16.410 --> 00:57:20.370
except in like a 1,200 page
study in dense academic German.

00:57:20.370 --> 00:57:22.530
And then it also begs the question

00:57:22.530 --> 00:57:25.600
who then exactly is this
reckoning with, right?

00:57:25.600 --> 00:57:27.913
Because for the most part,
the Germans of course,

00:57:28.772 --> 00:57:32.260
were not the victims of the
crimes of these patriarchs,

00:57:32.260 --> 00:57:34.004
and their businesses.

00:57:34.004 --> 00:57:35.269
- Right.

00:57:35.269 --> 00:57:37.560
- So, but, you know, surviving
forced and slave laborers

00:57:37.560 --> 00:57:40.343
and their heirs never get
any access to these details.

00:57:41.312 --> 00:57:42.145
- Yeah.

00:57:42.145 --> 00:57:44.870
- So it very much begs the
question, you know, and then,

00:57:44.870 --> 00:57:47.670
you know, when a company
or a family, again,

00:57:47.670 --> 00:57:49.926
doesn't do anything, you know,

00:57:49.926 --> 00:57:52.531
just goes to show that they don't, to me,

00:57:52.531 --> 00:57:54.110
it goes to show that it's not an honest

00:57:54.110 --> 00:57:57.230
and transparent reckoning
that they've initiated.

00:57:57.230 --> 00:58:00.850
- So your book is about
to come out in German also

00:58:00.850 --> 00:58:03.280
which is, you know, fascinating.

00:58:03.280 --> 00:58:07.370
So what do you imagine
the reaction is gonna be?

00:58:07.370 --> 00:58:11.583
You're gonna do a whole, you
know, book tour there you said.

00:58:11.583 --> 00:58:15.986
Like have you gotten any advance
sense of what the reaction?

00:58:15.986 --> 00:58:16.819
- No, no, I haven't.

00:58:16.819 --> 00:58:18.130
No, no, I mean, the
book is gonna come out.

00:58:18.130 --> 00:58:20.550
The German translation is
gonna come out May 5th.

00:58:20.550 --> 00:58:23.960
I mean the two translators
and the editor and myself

00:58:23.960 --> 00:58:27.725
worked extremely hard on
the German translation

00:58:27.725 --> 00:58:32.725
to be the best version
it, I mean, you know,

00:58:32.890 --> 00:58:35.130
the English language
version is also great,

00:58:35.130 --> 00:58:38.573
but the even better version of that one,

00:58:39.931 --> 00:58:44.360
and, you know, we really
worked on it day and night,

00:58:44.360 --> 00:58:49.312
particularly in January, December,
January, and early March.

00:58:49.312 --> 00:58:53.380
And, you know, I got a sense,

00:58:53.380 --> 00:58:55.580
actually the man who
inspired me to write the book

00:58:55.580 --> 00:58:57.950
or gave me the idea for the book

00:58:57.950 --> 00:59:02.520
is a German journalist who
wrote biographies on the Quandts

00:59:02.520 --> 00:59:07.029
and the Oetkers, and he
actually I'd sent him a,

00:59:07.029 --> 00:59:12.029
I'd send him a, yeah, an
advanced copy of the book,

00:59:14.320 --> 00:59:17.463
and he say, I woke up to this
morning with a note from him

00:59:17.463 --> 00:59:19.210
that he said he read it in one go.

00:59:19.210 --> 00:59:20.410
And he said, I learned a lot.

00:59:20.410 --> 00:59:21.950
And it's really well read,

00:59:21.950 --> 00:59:25.512
and really well told and
et cetera, et cetera.

00:59:25.512 --> 00:59:26.734
So that's, I mean, in a way,

00:59:26.734 --> 00:59:27.567
he's my most important critic

00:59:27.567 --> 00:59:32.060
since he inspired me to write
the book back in 2012, 2013,

00:59:32.060 --> 00:59:34.910
or gave me the idea that I
should write about this topic

00:59:34.910 --> 00:59:36.670
in a way, not explicitly,

00:59:36.670 --> 00:59:40.193
but through the interviews
I had with him at the time,

00:59:41.080 --> 00:59:44.216
I got the idea and, you know,

00:59:44.216 --> 00:59:46.630
in a way he's my most important critic.

00:59:46.630 --> 00:59:50.200
So the fact that he approved
of it already goes to show,

00:59:50.200 --> 00:59:51.723
I'm almost already very happy.

00:59:52.599 --> 00:59:53.432
- Yeah, that's fantastic.

00:59:53.432 --> 00:59:54.570
Okay, well, we're out of time,

00:59:54.570 --> 00:59:56.330
but I wanna say thank you so much

00:59:56.330 --> 01:00:00.160
for sharing your research
and telling us about

01:00:00.160 --> 01:00:04.003
this really important chapter in history.

01:00:04.003 --> 01:00:07.100
I wanna thank everyone in
the audience for coming.

01:00:07.100 --> 01:00:10.740
I wanna urge you all to please sign up

01:00:10.740 --> 01:00:14.730
to be on the YPSA mailing list.

01:00:14.730 --> 01:00:17.100
This is our last talk for this year,

01:00:17.100 --> 01:00:20.840
but we'll have many important
lectures next year too.

01:00:20.840 --> 01:00:24.410
And please join me in
thanking David de Jong

01:00:24.410 --> 01:00:26.670
for that great presentation today.

01:00:26.670 --> 01:00:27.730
- Thank you, Maurie.

01:00:27.730 --> 01:00:28.760
It was my pleasure.

01:00:28.760 --> 01:00:29.920
It was really nice to be here.

01:00:29.920 --> 01:00:31.591
- And good luck with.

01:00:31.591 --> 01:00:32.424
- Yeah, thank you very much.

01:00:32.424 --> 01:00:33.390
- Yeah, okay.

01:00:33.390 --> 01:00:34.680
Bye, everybody.

